1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,628
A pot plant on the window
grows bigger every week.

2
00:00:03,628 --> 00:00:07,650
A water plant in a fish tank
gives off tiny bubbles in the sun.

3
00:00:07,650 --> 00:00:10,383
Grass under a flat
stone turns pale yellow.

4
00:00:10,783 --> 00:00:12,030
Three small things.

5
00:00:12,030 --> 00:00:13,097
One big secret.

6
00:00:13,097 --> 00:00:16,640
Plants make their own food,
and they need light to do it.

7
00:00:17,090 --> 00:00:19,632
Diya saw the bubbles in
her cousin's fish tank.

8
00:00:19,632 --> 00:00:21,960
She asked: what gas is in those bubbles?

9
00:00:21,960 --> 00:00:24,329
And how did scientists
first work this out?

10
00:00:24,679 --> 00:00:26,391
Here is the short answer.

11
00:00:26,391 --> 00:00:27,713
The gas is oxygen.

12
00:00:27,713 --> 00:00:32,164
And it took many clever experiments,
over about two hundred years, to see

13
00:00:32,164 --> 00:00:33,333
the whole story.

14
00:00:33,833 --> 00:00:36,868
By the end, you will know the experiments
of Priestley, Ingenhousz, Sachs,

15
00:00:36,868 --> 00:00:39,454
Engelmann and van Niel, and the
full equation of photosynthesis.

16
00:00:40,104 --> 00:00:42,038
First, what is photosynthesis?

17
00:00:42,388 --> 00:00:43,654
Photo means light.

18
00:00:43,654 --> 00:00:45,158
Synthesis means making.

19
00:00:45,158 --> 00:00:47,738
So photosynthesis means making with light.

20
00:00:48,188 --> 00:00:51,166
Green plants take in
carbon dioxide and water.

21
00:00:51,166 --> 00:00:55,305
Using light energy, they make
sugar, and they give out oxygen.

22
00:00:55,305 --> 00:00:57,782
Almost all food on Earth starts here.

23
00:00:58,432 --> 00:01:02,100
You can test this yourself,
with three simple leaf tests.

24
00:01:02,450 --> 00:01:03,350
Test one.

25
00:01:03,242 --> 00:01:05,927
Take a leaf with green and white patches.

26
00:01:05,927 --> 00:01:07,317
Test it for starch.

27
00:01:07,317 --> 00:01:09,500
Only the green parts have starch.

28
00:01:09,500 --> 00:01:12,513
So chlorophyll, the
green pigment, is needed.

29
00:01:13,013 --> 00:01:13,913
Test two.

30
00:01:13,728 --> 00:01:17,198
Cover part of a leaf with black
paper and keep it in light.

31
00:01:17,198 --> 00:01:19,343
Only the uncovered part makes starch.

32
00:01:19,343 --> 00:01:20,599
So light is needed.

33
00:01:21,099 --> 00:01:21,999
Test three.

34
00:01:21,979 --> 00:01:26,611
Put half a leaf in a tube with cotton
soaked in KOH, potassium hydroxide.

35
00:01:26,611 --> 00:01:28,477
KOH soaks up carbon dioxide.

36
00:01:28,477 --> 00:01:30,248
That half makes no starch.

37
00:01:30,248 --> 00:01:32,114
So carbon dioxide is needed.

38
00:01:32,764 --> 00:01:37,226
Now go back to seventeen seventy, and
the first big experiment, by Joseph

39
00:01:37,226 --> 00:01:38,126
Priestley.

40
00:01:38,348 --> 00:01:39,248
Step one.

41
00:01:39,095 --> 00:01:42,011
He put a burning candle
under a glass bell jar.

42
00:01:42,011 --> 00:01:43,605
Soon the flame went out.

43
00:01:44,355 --> 00:01:45,255
Step two.

44
00:01:45,074 --> 00:01:47,344
He put a mouse in a closed bell jar.

45
00:01:47,344 --> 00:01:48,966
Soon it could not breathe.

46
00:01:49,716 --> 00:01:50,616
Step three.

47
00:01:50,517 --> 00:01:53,349
He placed a mint plant
inside the same bell jar.

48
00:01:54,099 --> 00:01:54,999
Step four.

49
00:01:54,907 --> 00:01:58,392
Now the candle kept burning,
and the mouse stayed alive.

50
00:01:59,142 --> 00:02:00,042
Step five.

51
00:01:59,954 --> 00:02:04,222
His idea: plants restore to the air
whatever breathing animals and burning

52
00:02:04,222 --> 00:02:05,269
candles remove.

53
00:02:05,269 --> 00:02:08,229
In seventeen seventy four,
he discovered oxygen.

54
00:02:08,979 --> 00:02:09,935
Remember this.

55
00:02:09,935 --> 00:02:11,400
Plants restore the air.

56
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:13,360
But Priestley missed one thing.

57
00:02:13,360 --> 00:02:14,260
Light.

58
00:02:13,755 --> 00:02:15,218
That was found by Jan Ingenhousz.

59
00:02:15,568 --> 00:02:17,154
He used a water plant.

60
00:02:17,154 --> 00:02:21,142
In bright sunlight, small bubbles
formed around its green parts.

61
00:02:21,142 --> 00:02:23,564
In the dark, there were no bubbles.

62
00:02:23,564 --> 00:02:25,236
So sunlight is essential.

63
00:02:25,686 --> 00:02:29,649
Around eighteen fifty four, Julius
von Sachs showed that green parts make

64
00:02:29,649 --> 00:02:31,709
glucose, usually stored as starch.

65
00:02:31,709 --> 00:02:36,111
He also found that chlorophyll sits in
special bodies, now called chloroplasts.

66
00:02:36,761 --> 00:02:38,950
Next, which colours of light work best?

67
00:02:38,950 --> 00:02:40,101
Engelmann found out.

68
00:02:40,451 --> 00:02:43,261
He split light with a
prism into its colours.

69
00:02:43,261 --> 00:02:48,004
Under them he placed a green alga,
Cladophora, in water with aerobic bacteria.

70
00:02:48,004 --> 00:02:50,750
Aerobic bacteria gather
where there is oxygen.

71
00:02:51,200 --> 00:02:54,698
The bacteria crowded mainly
in the blue and red light.

72
00:02:54,698 --> 00:02:57,635
So photosynthesis works
best in blue and red.

73
00:02:57,635 --> 00:02:59,961
This was the first action spectrum.

74
00:03:00,611 --> 00:03:02,106
One question was left.

75
00:03:02,106 --> 00:03:04,184
Where does the oxygen come from?

76
00:03:04,534 --> 00:03:07,654
Cornelius van Niel studied
purple and green bacteria.

77
00:03:07,654 --> 00:03:10,063
They use hydrogen sulphide, not water.

78
00:03:10,063 --> 00:03:12,288
They give out sulphur, not oxygen.

79
00:03:12,738 --> 00:03:16,800
So he said: hydrogen from some
compound reduces carbon dioxide.

80
00:03:16,800 --> 00:03:18,989
To reduce means to add hydrogen.

81
00:03:18,989 --> 00:03:21,734
In green plants, that compound is water.

82
00:03:22,334 --> 00:03:23,234
Your turn.

83
00:03:23,218 --> 00:03:28,026
In green plants, does the oxygen come
from carbon dioxide or from water?

84
00:03:28,026 --> 00:03:29,327
Pause and decide.

85
00:03:32,527 --> 00:03:33,427
From water!

86
00:03:33,264 --> 00:03:36,167
Later, experiments with
radioactive tracers confirmed it.

87
00:03:36,817 --> 00:03:38,388
Now, a worked example.

88
00:03:38,388 --> 00:03:40,824
Let's build the full equation and count.

89
00:03:41,174 --> 00:03:44,044
In: six carbon dioxide and twelve water.

90
00:03:44,044 --> 00:03:47,235
Out: one glucose, six
water and six oxygen.

91
00:03:47,685 --> 00:03:49,045
Count oxygen atoms.

92
00:03:49,045 --> 00:03:51,570
Six oxygen molecules hold twelve atoms.

93
00:03:51,570 --> 00:03:55,051
Twelve water molecules hold
exactly twelve oxygen atoms.

94
00:03:55,501 --> 00:03:58,431
So all the released oxygen
can come from water.

95
00:03:58,431 --> 00:04:01,408
That is why twelve water
are written, not six.

96
00:04:02,058 --> 00:04:03,520
Now, the exam trap.

97
00:04:03,870 --> 00:04:06,171
Many students think Priestley
showed that plants need light.

98
00:04:06,571 --> 00:04:09,043
But Priestley showed that
plants restore the air.

99
00:04:09,043 --> 00:04:11,834
It was Ingenhousz who showed
that sunlight is essential.

100
00:04:12,484 --> 00:04:13,757
Where is this asked?

101
00:04:13,757 --> 00:04:17,600
As match-the-scientist questions, and
statements on the source of oxygen.

102
00:04:18,200 --> 00:04:19,356
Exam level now.

103
00:04:19,356 --> 00:04:21,717
Match each scientist to the finding.

104
00:04:21,717 --> 00:04:22,922
Pause and solve.

105
00:04:28,322 --> 00:04:29,728
The answer is B.

106
00:04:30,228 --> 00:04:31,804
Priestley: plants restore air.

107
00:04:31,804 --> 00:04:33,304
Ingenhousz: light is needed.

108
00:04:33,304 --> 00:04:35,589
Sachs: glucose is made
and stored as starch.

109
00:04:36,089 --> 00:04:39,725
Engelmann used a prism and Cladophora,
and found that blue and red light

110
00:04:39,725 --> 00:04:40,625
work best.

111
00:04:41,023 --> 00:04:42,563
Here is your recap.

112
00:04:42,563 --> 00:04:44,146
Screenshot this card.

113
00:04:47,346 --> 00:04:49,532
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

114
00:04:49,932 --> 00:04:50,832
One.

115
00:04:50,278 --> 00:04:51,844
What did Priestley's bell jar show?

116
00:04:52,744 --> 00:04:53,644
Two.

117
00:04:53,102 --> 00:04:54,679
What did Engelmann's bacteria show?

118
00:04:55,579 --> 00:04:56,479
Three.

119
00:04:56,087 --> 00:04:58,778
Where does the oxygen in
photosynthesis come from?

120
00:04:59,878 --> 00:05:04,347
Next time: inside the leaf, where
exactly does this happen, and why are

121
00:05:04,347 --> 00:05:06,600
there four colours hiding in green?

122
00:05:06,600 --> 00:05:10,573
We find out in where photosynthesis
takes place and the pigments.
